                <ol class="story__steps">
                  <li data-step="0"><span class="tag">[1/5]</span><span>A thread is a line of turns: you ask, the agent works, you read what it did.</span></li>
                  <li data-step="1"><span class="tag">[2/5]</span><span>When a turn ends, the conversation and the code are saved together as a checkpoint.</span></li>
                  <li data-step="2"><span class="tag">[3/5]</span><span>Fork from any checkpoint. Each fork gets its own branch, and can run a different agent.</span></li>
                  <li data-step="3"><span class="tag">[4/5]</span><span>Each fork gets a lane. Quiet turns fold into a count, and every fork says where it stands: its agent, working or idle, and since when.</span></li>
                  <li data-step="4"><span class="tag">[5/5]</span><span>Keep one fork, and carry what it learned back into the thread you started from.</span></li>
                </ol>
                <div class="story__text">
                  <h3 class="h2 story__title">Lineage draws it beside the chat.</h3>
                  <p class="body">
                    Open the Lineage tab next to any thread. Hover a turn for its question, its reply and
                    the files it changed; click it to scroll the chat there, or click a fork to open it in
                    a tab. What comes back from a fork arrives as a summary, so the thread stays readable.
                  </p>
                  <ul class="caps">
                    <li>Put the files back as they were at any turn.</li>
                    <li>Each fork works on a branch of its own.</li>
                    <li>Switch agents between turns.</li>
                    <li>Checkpoints are git refs you can read without us.</li>
                  </ul>
